this week the case against the five men  accused of aiding the hijackers inch  forward with pre-trial hearings senior  investigative correspondent catherine  herridge was at ground zero on 9 11 and  has followed the case to the military  court in guantanamo bay cuba where she  joins us now catherine good morning  michelle this week lawyers argued over  the evidence and there is still no date  on the calendar for the actual trial to  begin  the five defendants including the  self-described architect of the attack  khalid sheikh mohammed are accused of  war crimes including conspiracy to  commit acts of terrorism hijacking and  the intentional murder of civilians  inside the high security court their  first appearance since the pandemic hit  the men appeared older some thinner  khalid sheikh mohammed stroked his long  white beard tinted orange on the sides  in the gallery a handful of 9 11 family  members it was a very emotional  experience for me it was  very moving very difficult  the biggest legal block is the men's cia  detention before they arrived at  guantanamo military prosecutors say they  are committed to a fair and just process  however long it takes dana catherine  herridge and guantanamo bay cuba thank  you catherine
 

